# P09 re-cut: port the prototype, one gate per screen

Agreed with the owner on 2026-10-29 after C32 took twenty review rounds while
the built v2 screens never matched the design. This supersedes the chunking
of C33–C34 in `IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md`; the specification
(`docs/architecture/ui-v2/`) and the core work of C27–C33 stand.

## The bar

- **The prototype is the spec.** `docs/architecture/ui-v2/prototype/` (Refined
  Native v2, version 134, checksums in `MANIFEST.sha256`) is the reference for
  every screen; the canvas is not consulted.
- **A screen is done when it matches its board.** Side by side at 1440 wide in
  light and dark (the board is drawn at 1440×900; where the display cannot
  give a 900px web area both sides are rendered at the window's measured web
  area, named in the composite's caption, 1440×800 on the current Mac), with
  real core data wired, and the fast checks (typecheck,
  Vitest, the affected Rust suites) passing. Parity is structural: regions,
  widths, spacing, type scale, tokens and component shapes. Content differs;
  accessibility corrections (focus rings, contrast, status regions) may deviate
  and are noted. The owner judges each composite.
- **Composites** live at `docs/planning/evidence/<chunk>/parity/<board>-<theme>.png`
  (prototype left, app right) with `notes.md` listing deviations, produced by
  `tools/parity/compose.py` (headless render of the prototype board beside the
  fixture app's own capture).
- **One heavy gate per chunk**, after the owner's parity approval: rebuild,
  native acceptance, the full Rust fixture suite and the Codex review, accepted
  at **8**. The phase review (after C44) is **9**; the C29 live Vulture row runs
  before it once the owner designates the L19 family.
- **No legacy views inside v2.** Each screen is new components over the
  existing core intents and `ShellSnapshot`; the legacy feature components stay
  untouched for the legacy shell until C44 removes it.
- **Cadence.** The shell is approved before any screen inside it. After that
  a chunk's composite is posted and work moves to the next chunk; the owner's
  notes are applied as they arrive.

## Chunks

| Chunk | Screen | Board(s) | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| C33 | core: case results and packs, call log, submissions | — | committed without a receipt; its two screens are superseded by C40/C41 |
| C35 | Shell | R2App any start | 56px header (title, 260px search, Explain switch = `showCalls`, Harness button); nav with product children and badges; status block; tenant menu 360px; palette 640px; tokens: text 13px, gaps, scrims, header/section/overlay sizes; Classic switch moves to Settings |
| C36 | Home | start=home | 3-column product cards with sparklines; Needs attention beside Quick actions |
| C37 | Inbox | start=inbox | fix-up: 430px list column, 3-column facts grid |
| C38 | Harness | start=harness (+ convo boards) | title band; 244px rail; transcript with 46px composer; 350px Decisions aside; own setup panels |
| C39a | Administration: Runtime Security | start=admin | section + tab strip; overview stat grid with sparkline; Playground form; generic tables over the management reads |
| C39b | Administration: other products | start=admin, start=review | the remaining products on the same pattern; the review sheet (620px) |
| C40 | Test Cases | start=cases | explain banner; one table card; packs and new-case dialogs (560px); detail sheet (520px) |
| C41 | API Client | start=api | full port: title row with chips; 286px request tree; open-request tabs; request sub-tabs; response pane |
| C42 | Academy | start=academy | prototype chrome around the hosted learner embed; composite judged on the chrome |
| C43 | Wizard | R2Onboard (all steps, mode=add) | re-port of the presentation over the existing state machine: pinned footer, 320/244px rail, section cards, segmented control, switch; model-driven extras housed in the prototype's cards |
| C44 | Legacy shell removal + phase review | — | on a branch; then the P09 review at 9 |

Each chunk brief under `docs/planning/chunks/` names the board, the regions
to port, the core intents and snapshot fields it reads, the native checks and
the test ids. The controller registers C35–C44 with a bar of 8.

## Fast loop

While building: `pnpm --filter @prisma-airs/desktop exec tsc --noEmit`,
`vitest run` for the desktop app, and `cargo test -p desktop-core --features
test-fixtures --test <suite>` for suites the change touches. The rebuild and
native run happen only at the gate, or when the composite needs a fresh capture.
Reviews run from a separate worktree so work continues meanwhile.

## First moves

1. Commit C33 as it stands (core, tests, the superseded screens).
2. Commit this re-cut: chunk briefs, controller registration, checks.
3. Build `tools/parity/compose.py` and run it on the current shell: the first
   composite is the baseline gap.
4. C35.
# API Client and Test Cases

Source: `R2App` (`REQ`, `hist`, `send`, `rawJson`, `CASES`, `packCases`,
`caseRows` lines 1033–1037, `runScan`). Chunk: C33. Both are re-skins of the
existing workbench (C14–C15) and library (C12) modules to the layout rhythm.
No new remote capability is introduced.

## API Client

- Title row, request tabs (named requests per product), method/URL line with
  the `get` colour, Send (32px), response pane with status, duration and
  body; history list of immutable snapshots (I5) from the existing workbench
  history.
- After Send, a compact `role="status"` region announces "<request> · <code>
  · <duration>"; the JSON body is outside the live region.
- Generic requests never bypass AIRS mutation authority (existing C15 rule):
  a request that would change configuration is refused by the core as today.
- The "Show the calls" panel reuses this response renderer.

## Test Cases

- Title row, filter by product, search, paged table (name, product, input,
  last result as verdict colour + icon + word), pack-sourced cases carry
  their pack name.
- **Runtime-only execution boundary** (prototype lines 1035–1037, parity
  matrix). A Runtime Security case offers "Open in Playground": it sets the
  Playground's prompt, direction and profile and the operator runs the scan
  there; the result is stored for the case as a local Playground run. A case
  for any other product offers "Inspect" only: it opens the specification
  and the recorded result, states which product runs it, and never
  dispatches a scan or claims execution. Red Team cases say "hand the spec
  to a scan"; no scan is started from this screen.
- Starter-pack cases selected in the wizard are written at onboarding
  completion as library entries with `Not run` results.

## Fixtures and tests

- Boards R2Api, R2Cases. Tests V2-R05, V2-T05; V2-T14 status region updates
  after Send with the body outside the live region; V2-T15 non-Runtime rows
  render Inspect, never Open in Playground, and clicking Inspect dispatches
  no scan intent (asserted on the mock runtime); V2-T16 paging and filter.
# C41 parity notes — API Client (R2App start=api)

Composites: prototype board left, fixture app right, both at the window's measured web area (1440 wide on this Mac), light and dark, captured by `tools/native-acceptance/acceptance_c41.py` against the bundle in `native-prepare.json` after a collection was opened through the native sheet and its request sent to the loopback service.

The board is the API Client: the title row with the Collection and Environment chips, the 286px request tree (Find request, +, folders with counts, requests with their methods), the open requests' tabs, the request line (method, URL, Send, the save state), the Params / Headers / Body / Auth / Variables / Preview sub-tabs over the request's columns and the response pane (status, Body / Headers / Details / History) at 40% on the right, the footer. C41 ports it as new components over the workbench intents; `WorkbenchView` and the legacy editor are not imported. The C14/C15 behaviour (local-first collections, drafts protected on this Mac, Save and Send taking the fields the editor holds, reviewed tenant-credential changes, immutable history) is the legacy editor's logic, ported.

## Deviations from the board, and why

- **Collection and environment are the core's.** The prototype scripts the built-in `scm` collection and an environment that "follows the tenant"; the port shows the collection the operator opened (name, folder or built-in) and the environment chosen from the collection's own (a select in the chip), since the core's environments are the collection's files and the private ones on this Mac.
- **The Collection chip opens a menu** (Refresh, Environments…, Secrets…, Schemas…, Import…, Git…, Close collection): the prototype's chip is a label. The C14 tools have no other home on the board; they open in a 620px docked sheet.
- **+ creates a request or a folder** through a small dialog (path under the collection); the prototype's + is disabled.
- **Request rows are editable** (the prototype's are read-only): Params and Headers are tables over the file's lines with a Text toggle; Body, Auth (the `[auth]`/`[variables]` TOML) and the Schema association are edited as text; Variables and Preview are read from the core's preview of the request as typed. The prototype's Auth and Variables tables script tenant credentials; the port shows what the file and the environment hold, never a value.
- **The URL is one field** with the `{{variable}}` prefix highlighted beside it rather than inside it.
- **The save state** sits in the request line's status region (Saved / Unsaved changes / New · not saved yet, and Send's completion "<request> · <code> · <duration>"), as the C33 tests require; the prototype shows a static "Saved".
- **Response body** is pretty-printed JSON with numbered lines by default and the exact text under Raw; Copy and Save response… are live (the prototype's Save is disabled).
- **History** lists the collection's runs newest first with the status pill, the request, the time and the duration; choosing one shows it in the pane as recorded (the prototype scripts two entries).
- **Footer** says Ready / Working…, the request count and the archives kept privately rather than "Saved · Shortcuts ⌘/".
- **Narrow windows.** Below 1180px the response pane drops under the request's columns; the prototype is fixed-width.

## Owner's judgement

The owner judged these composites (API Client: `parity/*.png`, prototype board left, fixture app right, light and dark) with the deviations above and approved them on 2026-10-30: "C39 C40 C41 C43 composites approved." This is the owner's judgement the gate runs on (D059); an objection from the owner reopens the parity judgement for this screen.
# 4. Managed HTTP enters native configuration authority

Date: 2026-10-16. Status: selected following owner candidate selection; independent G01 verdict
and implementation readiness are tracked in planning/status.md. Implements D014,D016 and C14/C15 requirements.

V1's workbench has real request-document and transport depth. Its redactions-based
redirect guard is not explicit tenant-credential classification, Live mode lacks
application destination binding, and its post-401 retry can resend a mutation.
The stricter v2 review/no-replay policy must be enforced below every Send path.

Keep RequestDocument reconciliation separate. Native execution admission captures
a fresh immutable effective request and explicitly distinguishes tenant-managed,
workbench-owned and anonymous credentials. Known managed reads need capability/
destination admission. Known mutations need exact Configuration review. Unknown
managed methods/paths are denied, even if presented as a read or generic draft.
Ordinary workbench-owned/anonymous HTTP remains supported.

Replace the editable SCM collection-ID credential gate with native-owned
capability registration. Imported IDs/references cannot authorize tenant secret
resolution. An edited token_url/request URL is admitted against the explicit
credential destination before secrets are obtained; an ID change never creates
a capability. Preserve operator-reviewed endpoint configuration separately.

Bind review to expanded URL/method/headers/body and file hashes, variables,
cookies, credential/context/destination and resource classification. Recheck
before dispatch; changed input/context/files require fresh review. Rust constructs
the actual request; renderer fields cannot mark it preapproved.

Managed redirects are disabled initially: a 3xx is shown, and following it is a
new prepared/admitted request. Generic redirects preserve bounded cookie/credential
origin policy. No automatic workbench resend follows HTTP401, refresh, reconnect,
lost response, import or history replay. Refresh can precede a new explicit Send.

The built-in catalogue has 193 request specifications, 81 with mutation methods;
this is metadata parity, not a claim of dedicated/live-qualified product support.
Tests cover imported/edited requests, unknown managed endpoints, direct IPC bypass,
ambiguous paths/auth headers, same/cross-origin redirects, stale cookie/file/
variable/context bindings, double Apply and post-401/lost-response no resend.

This is a stricter v2 product contract, not an allegation that v1 failed its own
contract. See [selected design](../architecture/DESIGN.md).

